course: dictionary
lesson: 13298
title: Adverbs that show a reason
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An adverb tells you how, when or where something happens. Gratuitously is an adverb, and it tells you that something is done without a good reason. «He gratuitously knocked over the sandcastle that the little girl had built.» Nobody needed the sandcastle to fall down, so the knocking over was not needed. That is what gratuitously means.
explain: Look for clues around the word. Here the clues are «knocked over» and «the little girl had built». Breaking someone's work for no reason is unkind, so gratuitously carries that unkind feeling too. Check your meaning by putting it in: «He knocked over the sandcastle for no good reason» still makes sense, so you are right.
explain: Reviewers use this word about films and books. «The film was gratuitously violent: the fight scenes went on far longer than the story needed.» The writer is saying there was more violence than the story needed, and that the violence did not help the plot.
fact: Gratuitously means without a good reason, in a way that is unnecessary.
fact: It often describes something unkind or shocking, such as «gratuitously cruel».
fact: Gratuitous is the adjective, and gratuitously is the adverb made from it.
fact: An old, formal meaning is free of charge, without payment, so «gratuitously» once meant "for nothing".
fact: A good check is to ask yourself whether the action was needed. If it was not, the word may be gratuitously.
exercise: «She gratuitously tore up the drawing her brother had worked on all week.» What does gratuitously mean here? | Carefully, with great care | *Without a good reason, in an unkind way | Quietly, in a whisper | Happily, with a big smile
exercise: A review says, «The film was gratuitously violent.» What does this suggest? | *There was more violence than the story needed | The film was very funny | The violence was short and gentle | There was no violence at all
exercise: Which word is closest in meaning to gratuitously in «He gratuitously ruined the game for everyone»? | *Needlessly | Carefully | Proudly | Slowly
test: «The king gratuitously punished the servant, although the servant had done nothing wrong.» What does gratuitously tell you about the punishment? | *It was unnecessary and unfair | It happened after dinner | It was a fair reward for hard work | It was a kind surprise
test: Swap your meaning into this sentence: «The bully gratuitously pushed the smallest boy into the mud.» Which meaning fits best? | Because it was raining | *For no good reason | To help the boy stand up | To win a prize
exam: When you meet «gratuitously» in a text, what should you ask yourself? | Does the word rhyme with another word? | Is the word written with a capital letter? | Does the word have more than five letters? | *Was there a good reason for this, or was it unnecessary?
